In C, you can terminate a program using the `exit()` function. This function is part of the standard library and is declared in the `stdlib.h` header file. When called, it causes the program to terminate immediately and return a status code to the operating system.

Here's an example of how to use the `exit()` function in a C program:
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");

    // Terminate the program and return a status code of 0
    exit(0);

    // This line will never be executed
    printf("This will not be printed.\n");

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the `exit(0)` statement is used to terminate the program after printing "Hello, World!". The status code 0 is passed to the operating system, indicating that the program executed successfully.
